{"name":"York Bisque","hex":"#f3d9c7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"york","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["to bowl a yorker at a batsman, especially to get a batsman out in this way."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To vomit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/york"},{"term":"bisque","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick creamy soup made from fish, shellfish, meat or vegetables.","A pale pinkish brown colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a pale pinkish brown colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bisque"}],"entries":[{"title":"York","blocks":[{"text":"York is a cathedral city in North Yorkshire, England, with Roman origins, sited at the confluence of the rivers Ouse and Foss. It has many historic buildings and other structures, including the minster, castle and city walls, all of which are Grade I listed. It is the traditional county town of Yorkshire, and the administrative centre of the City of York district. It is located 27 miles (43 km) north-east of Leeds, 90 miles (140 km) south of Newcastle upon Tyne and 207 miles (333 km) north of London. York's built-up area had a recorded population of 141,685 at the 2021 census."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/York_Minster_%282797690%29.jpg/330px-York_Minster_%282797690%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/York"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Clifford%27s_Tower%2C_from_south.JPG/500px-Clifford%27s_Tower%2C_from_south.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Clifford%27s_Tower%2C_from_south.JPG/500px-Clifford%27s_Tower%2C_from_south.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A